SecJev Use and Distribution Agreement
Version 1.0 — 23 September 2026

Copyright (c) 2026 the respective SecJev contributors, for their original
contributions only.

1. Scope and rights holders

This agreement covers the original SecJev contributions in releases that
expressly reference it: code, documentation, dataset compilation and original
annotations, and model adaptations, only to the extent the relevant contributor
holds rights that can be licensed. It does not claim ownership of upstream
datasets, model weights, software, individual facts or public-domain material.

Third-party components and source-derived records retain their own applicable
terms. Consult SOURCES.md, UPSTREAM.md and THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md where supplied,
the licenses/ directory, and record-level provenance. Inclusion in a SecJev
release does not turn third-party material into material licensed solely under
this agreement. This agreement does not withdraw permissions validly granted
for earlier releases under another license.

2. Permission for original SecJev contributions

Subject to this agreement, each contributor grants a worldwide, non-exclusive,
royalty-free copyright license to use, copy, study, modify and distribute that
contributor's covered contributions, including for research, education,
evaluation, training, fine-tuning and distillation. This grant does not itself
prohibit commercial use of those contributions; section 4 governs uses that
also involve upstream rights or conditions. Permissions remain available for
the duration of the licensed rights while the applicable conditions are met.

3. Attribution, modifications and redistribution

When distributing covered contributions or modifications, retain this agreement,
applicable copyright and attribution notices, source references and required
third-party license texts. Identify the SecJev release used, describe material
changes, and distinguish modified releases from the original project. Preserve
source-to-record mappings, or provide an equivalent usable provenance mapping,
when distributing source-derived data.

Redistribute upstream material only to the extent its own terms or a separate
authorization permit. If permission for a particular act of redistribution is
unclear, this agreement does not supply that permission; obtain clarification
or distribute acquisition/transformation instructions instead of that material.
Do not represent this agreement as removing upstream conditions. Do not impose
restrictions on a recipient's independent rights in third-party or public-domain
material. Derivatives may carry additional terms for new contributions only
where those terms respect the existing rights and obligations.

4. Source-specific conditions and commercial use

The complete corpus has multiple sources and no blanket unrestricted commercial
clearance. In particular:

- ToN-IoT's official terms grant academic research use and require permission
  from its author for commercial use. This agreement grants neither that
  permission nor a broader redistribution right on the author's behalf.
- CIC requires dataset and paper attribution for its permitted redistribution.
- VeReMi material licensed under CC BY 4.0 retains attribution, change-notice
  and other requirements of that license, and its independently granted rights.
- LANL material covered by a public-domain dedication remains public domain.
- MIT- and Apache-licensed upstream software and model components retain their
  license texts and applicable notices; simulator licenses must not be assumed
  to determine every right in their outputs or input datasets.

The source-specific register gives details and links to the original terms.
Where a proposed use requires additional authorization, obtain it from each
relevant rights holder. SecJev contributors cannot grant upstream permissions.

Training on data does not, by this agreement alone, automatically assign the
data's license to every resulting weight or output, or establish that every
such weight or output is cleared for commercial use. Model releases must identify
their upstream components and training sources. Users must assess the terms
applicable to their actual data use, model use and redistribution. This agreement
does not assert that ordinary model outputs are owned by SecJev or must carry
this agreement merely because the model produced them.

5. Priority and reserved rights

Upstream terms govern upstream material; this agreement governs only the rights
granted by SecJev contributors. It must not be read to narrow CC BY, MIT, Apache,
CC0 or other independently granted upstream permissions. Statutory exceptions
and uses that require no permission remain unaffected. No trademark rights or
right to imply endorsement are granted. No additional patent license is granted
here; independently applicable upstream patent grants remain unaffected.

6. No warranties; responsibility for use

To the extent permitted by applicable law, covered contributions are supplied
as available without warranties, including as to accuracy, fitness for purpose
or non-infringement. Contributors are not liable for losses arising from their
use to the extent such liability may lawfully be excluded. This agreement does
not exclude rights or liabilities that applicable law makes non-excludable.

7. Noncompliance

Permissions under this agreement terminate for a use that violates its
conditions. They are reinstated when the violation is remedied within 30 days
of the user's discovery, or with the relevant contributor's written permission.
This does not change upstream licenses or remedies available under applicable
law. A legally ineffective provision does not invalidate the remainder where
the remainder can operate consistently with this agreement's scope.

8. Versions and inquiries

This version applies to releases that expressly adopt it. Later versions do not
automatically change the terms of existing copies. The accompanying Chinese
translation is for convenience; this English text controls in a discrepancy.
Project inquiries: https://github.com/UESTC1010/SecJev/issues

This is a project-specific agreement, not a claim of OSI approval or a substitute
for permissions that only upstream rights holders can grant.
